well of course the Conservatives are  enjoying their 80 seat majority it was a  tricky election night for the Liberal  Democratic Party choice Winston began  the campaign sitting opposite me in this  very studio claiming that she was a  realistic candidate for Prime Minister  on Thursday night she lost her seat and  so just 11 MPs returned for her party  well that means the Lib Dems are left  with one less MP than we had after the  2017 election and 10 less than at the  start of the campaign because all of the  people that had defected from other  parties failed to win their seats well  joining us now to discuss where they go  next is the party's joint acting leader  said Davey thank you very much good  morning on a programme let's address the  elephant in the room first there's a  vacancy at the top of your party are you  planning on running for the leadership  again as we just had a very tough  election I'm delighted to have won my  seat but I'm talking to colleagues  who've had some really tough times and  lost their seat what we're going to do  is as Acting leader I'm going to ensure  that we review the general election  properly learn those lessons and make  sure we're fit to oppose this Boris  Johnson government as we is in our job  to do so that's the role I'll be  focusing in on in my role as Acting  leader and can you give us a bit of a  idea of a time frame for this when is  the leadership election likely to take  place well the board of the party is  going to meet in January and is going to  set the timetable for the leadership  election as acting leader our bill  should be supporting the board but  focusing in on talking to our members  clearly not just our party's members but  supporters and many people who want a  progressive centre party are deeply  disappointed at the moment and I think  is vital they see politicians omitting  where mistakes are made but also  standing up to this quite nationalistic  right-wing conservative government  that's been elected to just be crystal  clear you're not ruling yourself in or  out it's too early for that we've got to  focus on ensuring we learn the lessons  properly in a quite a deep profound way  I mean one of the lessons I have to say  is it can be very difficult for the  Democrats to make progress when there is  a hard left leader they party  1983 with Michael foot we found in  spades in 29 with Jeremy Corbyn if you  look at the conservative leaflets in  libel Democrat consensus it wasn't so  much get brexit done they were talking  about they were saying if you vote  little democrat you'll catch enemy  Corbin and that fear factor was a  massive issue in the general ledger at  the same time though many people would  look at this and think this is a perfect  opportunity for a party the center  ground to come through when many people  feel Boris Johnson is a right-wing Prime  Minister and then you have an unpopular  leader on the left wing  surely this gives you space in the  center ground that you've failed to  grasp well we clearly fail to grasp it  and we need to learn the lessons of why  but I'm pointing out to you that there  were a lot of liberal conservatives who  read don't like brexit don't like Boris  Johnson but they actually fear Jeremy  Corbyn even more and you don't have to  believe me look at the leaflets that the  Conservative Party were putting out in  the key seats we were trying to win and  they played on that fear factor of  Jeremy Corbyn and what I hope is that we  can see Labour Party reform because we  do need to work cross-party we need to  cooperate we try that very much in the  last Parliament as we try to get a  people's vote we didn't quite get the  full cooperation that we needed we try  that at this this election we were  working with in the Green Party Pleiku  Murray we try to work across across  party boundaries but in the  first-past-the-post with a very hard  left and popper Leader the Labour Party  it proved very very difficult now when  you're on the show before the election I  asked what you would have done  differently to Joe Swinson if you've  been elected leader you just have a  quick look at what you told me and  before this you said that you had done  nothing differently I think she's been  doing a fantastic job  I think her leadership on brexit has  been inspiring what would you actually  have done differently well I would  certainly have done exactly what Joe did  on the BEC see question we are a  pro-european party you believe Britain  should remain in the EU and I'm very  proud that Joe let a party where she  made that a central position I think the  question is more about what you'd have  done differently not the same well as  acting lead  I want to work out what went wrong and  what kind of work and well let's let us  look at this in detail I was at the  board meeting yesterday in Liberal HQ  where we have set up a general election  review we'll look at all the details we  won't no stone will be left unturned  clearly we can bust of make mistakes we  wouldn't have had the result that we did  but all I'm saying is judging from what  I've already heard from members and from  candidates and from party activists the  Conservative Party beat us in many seats  were you expecting to win because they  played on the fear of Jeremy Corbyn that  that's just a fact  they'll be fed into the review will also  have to own up to the mistakes that we  made I'm not denying that what are they  because it does feel a little bit like  you know you're blaming Jeremy Corbyn  you say you would have done anything  differently on brexit than Joe Swinson  so what were the mistakes you can't just  blame external factors oh but you're  asking to me for me to rush to early  judgment and I'm saying I'm not going to  you must have a sense after you you've  been out door locking you know you  you've you've been out you must have a  sense of what well let me give you let  me give me one example which I think  will be picked up in review I expect I  think we didn't get the other messages  over about what Liberal Democrats stand  for and we didn't get beyond the stock  brexit position so for example we've got  a very strong position on climate change  we we beef in fact Joe and I both said  during the general election that in many  ways tacking the climate emergency was  even bigger than stopping brexit but I'm  not sure if that very strong liberal  Democrat record and that strong set of  libel democrat polices and climate  change got through to enough voters and  I think one of the challenges we we fail  to manage is making sure that people  understood the little hats aren't just  the stock brexit party we've got many  other very attractive policies which  should have brought more people to to  vote to it what about the policy to  revoke article 52 cancel Breck say if  you won a majority was that a  distraction well I think I want to look  at the data on that because we did see  data where it was bringing people to  others as well as some data saying it  wasn't working and but because I  actually believe in evidence I believe  an evidence-based poll  it's why I don't think we should leave  the EU because the evidence is actually  but better off in the EU I want to see  the evidence before I read the evidence  is that you only 111 MPs isn't it it was  not a good night I'm not going to  pretend but there were glimmers of hope  let's remember that our vote share went  up we're now second in many more seats  than we were before the last election so  I think libel emma-crats can rebuild I  think we can come back strong and I  think if we focus on those very positive  messages defending human rights for  example we've got a government that's  been elected on a mandate to attack the  human rights legislation to actually go  after the the courts and reform the  courts attacking I think the  Independence of the judiciary on things  like judicial review we've got good  liberal value issues which we can  champion and I tell you one thing that's  going to happen very soon we're going to  see I hope and expect the report on  Russian interference in British  elections actually published I think  that would be very interesting and it  will be a Liberal Democrats who will be  holding Boris Johnson in person  to account for his links to Russian  business people what's the biggest  mistake of the election campaign backing  the election in the first place from the  Liberal Democrats it seems like it was  an act of political suicide and  registered now I think that's not  looking at what the dilemma was and  lemon was this we had tried time and  time again to get a people's vote to get  a referendum so that people have the  final say we had 17 attempts to that but  unfortunately the Labour Party didn't  show up in the numbers that were  required and therefore we then faced  with the Boris Johnson withdrawal bill  getting a majority with 19 Labour MPs  supporting it and there was a real fear  that that withdrawal agreement would get  through the House of Commons and so the  dilemma was this do we have an election  after Britain had legally been drawn  from the EU with ruderal Act or did we  have one before that was the choice and  you know we were faced with this I think  really meaningful meaning the slogan of  get brexit done but if we'd not had an  election we'd have had some sort of  other  meaningless solution slogan like let's  move on and so it was a real difficult  judgement to make and I think people  were moment are  not seeing what actually was ahead in  the face of the British Parliament at  the time there one of the people of  course he was paid perhaps the ultimate  price for that decision is Joseph winds  themselves she lost her seat do you feel  sorrowful absolutely the whole party is  a deeply upset and disappointed Joe was  a friend and a colleague and I want her  back in Parliament as soon as possible  she has so much to offer British  politics and you know our faults are  with her and her family but we know that  she has a bright future and we thank her  for what she did and I hope that we can  ensure that what she argued for and her  legacy on the prexy issue we can  continue because Liberal Democrats will  continue to approach brexit in  Parliament I'm sure he'll get his  legislation now the Drule agreement will  go through but now the hard part starts  for Boris Johnson could serve disc I do  not believe they can get brexit done in  the next year as they said they've done  they've told voters and leavers and  remains they'll get back see done next  year they won't and your words say about  Joseph Winston where perhaps not quite  knew it why everybody who watched those  results coming in we saw the video of  Nicola Sturgeon the leader of the SNP  celebrating the result when Joyce wins  then I lost I seek I look at the screen  I'm sure you've seen this what what's  your reaction she's not very dignified  as she and I think that approach to  politics are taking gleaned someone  else's defeat is in the way that she  does a very personal way isn't  appropriate for the First Minister fond  okay Devi thank you very much for being  on the program this morning

